    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "E. BROWN DET\nLENGTH ON DECK 240 PIN\nBREADTH OF BEAM... 43 FT\nWHOLE DEPTH TIN\nEntered aconding to ael of Congres in cho yous 1852 by N. Carin in the Clands Offin of the Distil Cause of Nam Yot\nJa Mosors N. I. gE. Griswold, Cuners g. W. H. Will Builder. this print of the\nNEW YORK CLIPPER SHP \"CHALLENGE\nROBT. H. WATERMAN, COMMANDER\nis Respoctfully dedicatodby the Publisher.\n2.006. TONS REGISTER\n3 DECKS\nSPREAD OF CANVAS. 12,780 YDS",
      "CLIPPER SHIP \"CHALLENGE\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print.\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Mariners Museum, Newport News, Virginia.\nWilliam H. Webb launched the clipper ship \"Challenge\" in May, 1851, in New York City. She\nwas extreme in every way. Her spars were long, her spread of canvas enormous and her V-bottom\nthe sharpest of any clipper afloat. Her reputation too was extreme. Robert Waterman, Captain\nof the \"Challenge\" had an infamous background which added to the difficulty of gathering a crew\nfor the hazardous trip around Cape Horn. With a crew of sixty-four men it took the \"Challenge\"\n109 days to reach California. Mutiny had broken out and drastic measures were used to enforce\nobedience. Newspapers in San Francisco erupted into a series of virulent accusations by the crew\nagainst the Captain. Although he was later exonerated, the name of \"Challenge\" had been dis-\ncredited. In 1859 she made her last passage under the American flag. The principal error in her\ndesign was her hollow waterline which sapped her power and slowed her passage through the water.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the early clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to aul tae lar places or the world... to dapan •\n.. to Australia... and, in the days of the\ngreat gold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California.\nOur current equipment... the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century... consists of the finest\ncommercial aircraft ever developed-aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record\nfor safety, speed and service.\n\"CLIPPER TRADE MARK-REG. US PAT OFFICE - COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS INC\n2000.058.03/6\nLITHO IN USA - 8739-0393"
    ],
